# Clean Code rule

Based on Robert C. Martin (_Clean Code_) and Kent Beck (_Tidy First?_). The full reference is the
`clean-code` skill; TypeScript specifics are in the `typescript-craftsmanship` skill.

## Principles

- **Duplicated knowledge is a bug; similar-looking code is a hint.** When the same business rule
  appears twice, move it now into the context that owns it. When code only looks alike, wait for
  the third occurrence and check it is the same concept.
- **One responsibility** per function, component and module. A name that needs "and" asks for a
  split.
- **Names reveal intent** and come from the glossary in `CONTEXT.md`.
- **Guard clauses** over nested `if`/`else`. Nesting depth ≤ 3.
- **Small units.** Cognitive complexity ≤ 15 per function. Components ≤ 200 lines, other files ≤
  300 lines, comments and blank lines excluded. A file already over its limit must not grow; leave
  it smaller when you touch it.
- **No magic values.** A named constant, with a comment that gives its source.
- **No commented-out code** and no `console.log` (use `src/lib/logger.ts`).
- **Comments carry information:** a module header, a TSDoc contract on every export, a "why" with
  its source. Never a restatement of the code (`docs/engineering/code-documentation.md`).

## Errors

- Parse at the boundary with Zod; inside, trust the types.
- Never swallow an error: log it, wrap it or rethrow it.
- Expected failures are values (a discriminated union), mapped exhaustively at the edge.

## Enforcement

The size and complexity limits are prose today. ESLint `max-lines`, `max-lines-per-function` and
`complexity`, with bulk suppressions as a ratchet, are planned
(`docs/adr/0007-architecture-enforcement-in-stages.md`). SonarCloud reports duplication and
complexity; it is advisory (`CLAUDE.md`).

## Agent behaviour

- Do not explain unclear code with a comment: rename or extract it first.
- Tidy in a separate structural commit, never mixed with a behaviour change.
- Do not reformat code you are not otherwise changing.
